# Line endings are not cosmetic here. `check_surface.sh` compares the symbols in the built
# library against `foundationdb.allow` line by line, so a CRLF checkout makes every listed
# symbol miss its match — and the check then reports the same 19 symbols as both unlisted
# and unused, which reads as a total surface violation rather than as a checkout artefact.
# It cost a debugging round on Windows to find that, so the rule is written down instead.
#
# Every shell script here is also run under `sh`, which reads `#!/bin/sh\r` as an
# interpreter named `/bin/sh\r` and reports "bad interpreter".
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.allow         text eol=lf
ci.sh           text eol=lf
*.c             text eol=lf
*.h             text eol=lf
*.cpp           text eol=lf
*.lean          text eol=lf

# The per-suffix lines above were not enough, and the reason took five incidents to see.
# `core.autocrlf` was `true` in this repository's own config, and the rules only ever reached
# files someone had thought to list. Anything unlisted -- `Containerfile`, both licences,
# `fly/fly.toml`, `.clangd` -- still checked out CRLF.
#
# The second half is what hid it. `git add --renormalize` writes LF into the *index*, and the
# working tree keeps what it had until the files are checked out again. So `git ls-files
# --eol` read `i/lf w/crlf` -- correct in the repository, wrong on disk -- and it is the copy
# on disk that `sh` runs and that `check_surface.sh` compares line by line. Adding rules
# looked like a fix and fixed nothing that already existed.
#
# So the default is stated once, for everything, rather than extended a suffix at a time.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Real binaries, which the line above must not touch.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.parquet       binary
*.zst           binary
*.jar           binary
